## Soundtrack architecture

**Story frame:** Eli, an aging traveling salesman, has spent his life converting disappointment into stories about pirates, sailors, and escape. Mara is his wife; their children eventually become the adults who must decide whether to reject or inherit his imaginative life. The musical remains sung-through: no dialogue, with instrumental transitions carrying scene changes.

The pirate motif develops in three stages:

1. **Act I, childhood fantasy:** bright, theatrical, literal pirates.
2. **Act I into Act II, adult dream:** the pirate identity becomes Eli’s escape from work, debt, aging, and failure.
3. **Act II ending, inherited imagination:** the family keeps the imaginative freedom while abandoning Eli’s self-deception.

All treatments retain each song’s original melody, harmonic identity, and recognizable form. Any cuts, repeats, transpositions, or vocal redistribution would be handled under the appropriate cover and dramatic-performance permissions.

---

# Act I — Child fantasy becoming adult dream

| # | Buffett song / form | Story function and singer/POV | Arrangement, tempo, and transition |
|---:|---|---|---|
| 1 | **“Jolly Mon Sing” — Full** | Young Eli invents the pirate world that will govern his imagination. Child Eli leads; children’s ensemble becomes his crew; adult Eli briefly observes the memory. | Bright calypso-shanty opening: acoustic guitar, ukulele, accordion, hand percussion, bass, brass, and children’s voices. Keep the buoyant original character. Ship-bell and rolling-tom figures establish the recurring “tide” motif. |
| 2 | **“Delaney Talks to Statues” — Full** | The child discovers that ordinary objects, statues, and landmarks can become companions. Young Eli’s POV, with silent ensemble figures animating his fantasy. | Gentle folk-rock with fingerpicked guitar, clarinet, celeste, brushed drums, and muted trumpet. The pirate world becomes less literal and more psychological. End with the child’s guitar figure handed to adult Eli. |
