There is **no fundamental reason** 30B cannot handle every Hindsight LLM function. I held it back because evidence is incomplete and its quality did not yet beat 8B:

- **30B:** 10/13 semantic passes; retained transient state and merged distinct count/name facets. Thinking mode did not fix this.
- **8B:** 28/28 on the larger retain corpus, with much lower memory use.
- 30B reflect/tool behavior was not tested.
- 30B used ~16.8 GB and was tested mostly in isolation. Co-residency with embeddings, reranking, and another model still needs proof.

**Recommended local topology:**

- **Qwen3-8B OMLX:** retain, four parallel lanes. Proven quality and roughly 720 requests/hour.
- **Qwen3-30B-A3B OMLX:** consolidation and deduplication, one or two lanes. Faster and stronger for cross-fact decisions.
- **30B reflect:** acceptable as a low-volume lane, but test it before enabling broadly.
- Grammar-constrained JSON plus deterministic validation:
  - reject transient browser/session facts;
  - reject count/name facet merges;
  - require exact UUIDs and source grounding;
  - allow one corrective retry, then quarantine.

This is better than putting all work through one 30B queue: retain stays fast while consolidation cannot block it. If the co-resident 8B + 30B resource soak passes, this is my preferred self-hosted design; if not, use **30B everywhere with the validators**, because its isolated throughput is sufficient.

